      "name": null,
      "problem": "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2(x-1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{2}{4})(x-1),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=1$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(1,0).$",
